From 7ced3d00f7d01d0f9a1e1f7c8a7179d8d96df611 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex Booster <webdevbooster@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 17:31:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Hold the two new doctrine clauses with a test that bites

The clauses landed in the previous commit are the highest-leverage prose in
the product and nothing asserted them. Every other load-bearing sentence in
that file has a test standing over it - the dialect clause, the company
absence, the 4 KB budget - and a sentence with no test is a sentence the
next edit deletes without anyone noticing.

Six assertions, both halves, because either one alone leaves the defect
standing: an entry is not proof of the present; an answer resting on a
stored entry goes to the thing itself first; an unchecked answer says it is
unchecked; broken and out of date are two findings; naming which one it is
is not the reader's job; and not knowing which yet is itself sayable.

PROVED NOT VACUOUS rather than assumed. I put the pre-change file back in
place and ran the test against it:

  doctrine.rs:436 panicked - "an entry must not be offered as proof of the
  present" - exit non-zero, then restored (template sha256 5af6d03faa570653,
  which is the digest the rendered file's first line now carries).

app/README.md's crate total moves with it. docs-claims.js check 3 counts
every #[test] under src/ and tests/ and refuses a README that disagrees, so
1034 -> 1035 and "1030 direct" -> "1031 direct" is part of this change, not
a follow-up.

  cargo test -p richos-core: 1036 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored
    (1031 direct + 4 ignored + 5 doc-tests)
  docs-claims.js: crate totals now read richos-core=1035+5, and the suite's
    only failure is the one already on main before I started (startup_exec.rs
    is named in no README section). I did not touch it.
